    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter today\u2019s slate with a clear focal point in the outfield: **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (82.13) and **Christian Yelich** (74.33). **Pete Crow-Armstrong** sits as our highest-rated starter, benefiting significantly from a lead-off spot against a favorable matchup with Tyler Phillips (4.51 xERA). His dynamic platoon advantage and current order placement make him an elite option in our pursuit of stolen bases, our scarcest category. Meanwhile, **Christian Yelich** remains a primary play against Singer; his projection is buoyed by a strong platoon edge and the high-leverage #2 spot in the order. Despite a tough draw against an elite arm, **Yordan Alvarez** (71.69) remains mandatory; even with a platoon disadvantage, his elite 0.450 xwOBA provides an efficiency floor that few others can match.\n\nThe optimizer faced its toughest decision at the utility slot, ultimately favoring **Junior Caminero** (61.33) over **Pedro Ramirez** (49.19). While **Pedro Ramirez** showed promise with a 49.19 efficiency score, the combination of his lower batting order (#7) and the lack of a clear platoon advantage kept him glued to the bench. Conversely, despite a moderate SP skill rating for MacKenzie Gore, **Junior Caminero** retains a superior base projection and the stability of the #3 spot in the lineup. Our benching of **Colt Keith** (46.34) was similarly calculated; facing a formidable arm like G. Williams (2.89 xERA) significantly suppressed his utility, pushing him below our internal efficiency floor for high-stakes starts.\n\nRisks today are concentrated in our \"Lineup Pending\" designations, specifically regarding **Christian Encarnacion-Strand** (53.05) and **Chase Meidroth** (54.04). Both hover near our marginal start threshold, and their values are sensitive to where they land in the batting order. If either drops lower than the assumed #7 spot, their already fragile scores could dip below our 40.0 floor, at which point we will prioritize game-cap preservation. We are closely monitoring weather and late lineup scratches for **Kazuma Okamoto** (58.98) and **Josh Smith** (64.31), who are currently well-positioned to capitalize on Randy Dobnak\u2019s high 4.62 xERA. Provided the lineups solidify as projected, we remain confident in this high-ceiling, high-speed construction.",
